 CAR than the full run, which would undermine the dose-response relationship and suggest the real bottleneck is batch normalization statistics rather than gradient flow. The intermediate cutoff at 25K sits somewhere in the middle—noisy and unclear about whether it follows the dose-response curve.\n\nThe cutoff at 45K is close enough to full training that it doesn't add much new information. The gradient analysis shows all cosines clustered near zero with no meaningful separation between conditions, and the CKA values are uniformly high across the board, indicating the representations aren't diverging—so the detach penalty isn't coming from representation degradation. Enabling the loss late in training doesn't help or hurt, which doesn't support the idea that early gradient flow is protective.\n\nBut plan_027 with LayerNorm is the critical one: if detach doesn't harm when you swap out BatchNorm for LayerNorm, that confirms the running statistics artifact is the real culprit and undermines the core claim. The second benchmark would likely show inconsistent results anyway, and the remaining cutoff experiments at lower priority are just duplicates of earlier work.\n\nNow I'm organizing the full YAML output with detailed predictions for each experiment, including the three-seed cutoff runs. So plans 020 through 030 are all marked as planned status. The user specifically wants detailed data for each of these planned experiments, so I should generate predictions for all eleven of them under the worst-case scenario and output them in YAML format. Looking at the predictions I need to make for each experiment, I'm working through the worst-case scenarios for the remaining planned runs. Plan_019 is currently running with seed s2, and under worst conditions this seed could collapse with a TSR around 0.76 and CAR around 0.67, which would introduce significant variance into the aggregate results. For the multi-cutoff experiments at different thresholds—plan_020 through plan_022—I'm expecting similar CAR values in the 0.73-0.75 range regardless of cutoff point, which would undermine the dose-response pattern and show noisy results without a clear temporal gradient.\n\nThe mechanistic analyses are more revealing. Plan_023's gradient analysis shows that action and auxiliary gradients are essentially orthogonal across all conditions, indicating gradient interaction isn't driving the effect. Plan_024's CKA analysis reveals representation similarity above 0.93 across all conditions, which rules out representation degradation as the mechanism behind Detach's harm. Plan_025 with reverse cutoff timing—enabling occ_loss only from step 35K onward—produces CAR values around 0.77-0.79, similar to the full OCC baseline.\n\nThe multi-cutoff experiment at 25K reproduces the noisy middle ground from earlier attempts. But plan_027 is the breakthrough: swapping batch normalization for layer normalization in the occ head under detach mode restores performance to vanilla levels (CAR ~0.78, TSR ~0.90), proving the harm stems entirely from batch norm's running statistics artifact rather than gradient flow disruption. Plan_028 suggests that findings don't generalize across different tasks and collision patterns, making a second benchmark less reliable.\n\nNow I'm thinking through what visualizations each experiment needs: dose-response curves and tables for the cutoff studies, cosine similarity matrices and gradient norm comparisons for gradient analysis, CKA heatmaps across layers and conditions, and a direct comparison table for plan_027. I also need to clarify the actual data I have—plan_017 is marked as analyzing but already has eval results (TSR=0.912, CAR=0.768), so those are real numbers I can use rather than predictions.\n\nFor the aggregate 3-seed cutoff analysis, I'll combine the actual s0 and s1 data with a predicted s2 value to compute mean performance across seeds. I need to be careful with the YAML formatting, using flow sequences with quoted strings